The Intersection of Environmental Law and Indigenous Rights in India 
Abstract 
India is a country where many Indigenous communities reside as custodians of ecological 
wisdom and biodiversity, who have historically preserved nature through sustainable living. 
However, environmental laws enacted to conserve nature frequently become tools of exclusion 
and displacement for these very communities. This article critically examines the intersection 
of environmental laws and Indigenous rights in India. It explores constitutional safeguards, 
legislative frameworks, judicial interpretation, and global parallels. This article argues for a 
harmonised legal landscape where environmental protection does not come at the cost of 
indigenous autonomy. This article reveals structural and procedural gaps in the Indian legal 
system. It offers policy suggestions to bridge these gaps and advocate for a future that respects 
ecological integrity and Indigenous dignity. 
Keywords: Environmental Law, Indigenous Rights, Adivasi Rights, Forest Rights Act (FRA), 
Tribal Communities, Environmental Justice. 
Introduction 
India is home to over 705 recognised Scheduled Tribes, which is more than 8.6% of India’s 
population or approximately 10.4 crore people. These Indigenous communities, often called 
Adivasis, have lived in harmony with their natural surroundings, like forests, rivers, and lands 
for generations. Their cultures and spiritual beliefs are deeply connected to nature which 
reflects a form of ecological understanding that predates modern conservation practices. 
Unfortunately, the relationship between environmental laws and Indigenous people's rights has 
always been tense in India. 
Most of the environmental regulations enacted after independence tend to focus heavily on the 
conservation of the environment, which leaves local communities out of conversation. When 
protected areas, wildlife sanctuaries and development zones are established, the area around 
becomes a graveyard of Indigenous identity. Widespread displacement, loss of traditional 
livelihood and dilution of rich cultural practices of the tribal population are just some of the 
consequences of such establishments. Ironically, the very people who have historically been 
the caretakers of these areas are labelled as encroachers.  
This article takes a closer look at how environmental laws have developed in India and their 
real impact on the rights and lives of these indigenous communities. By analysing the 
legislation, judicial interpretation and real-world case studies, this article aims to uncover 
whether the legal framework of India genuinely supports the principles of environmental 
justice or if it inadvertently sacrifices social equity for ecological preservation. 
Historical Context 
The Indigenous way of Life 
Historically, the Indigenous tribes of India have lived in harmony with nature, practising 
sustainable subsistence methods such as shifting cultivation (jhum), pastoralism, hunting, 
gathering, and the use of medicinal plants. Customary laws and unwritten ethical norms 
governed these practices. Unlike extractive industrial practices, tribal livelihoods never led to 
large-scale environmental degradation. 
Indigenous knowledge systems (IKS) show a nuanced understanding of local biodiversity, 
weather patterns and soil management. Despite their environmental significance, these systems 
never really recognised in colonial and postcolonial policies. 
Colonial Forest Laws and the Seeds of Alienation 
British rule redefined the forest as state property in the Indian Forest Act of 1865 and its later 
version in 1927. These laws criminalised traditional tribal activities and declared forests as 
revenue-generating resources. The colonial state prioritised timber extraction over human 
