The Constitution of India provides both the mandate to protect the environment and safeguard 
the rights of Scheduled Tribes. This dual mandate creates a legal landscape where rights can 
either be harmonized or placed in conflict.  
• Article 21 guarantees the right to life, which has been interpreted to include the right to 
a healthy environment (Subhash Kumar v. State of Bihar, 1991). 
• Article 46 mandates the state to promote the economic and educational interests of 
scheduled tribes. 
• The Fifth Schedule governs the administration of the Scheduled Areas and provides 
autonomy through Tribal Advisory Councils. 
• Article 244 and the Sixth Schedule ensure special governance structures for tribal 
majority regions. 
• Article 48A (Directive Principles) and 51A (g) (Fundamental Duties) impose 
responsibilities on the state and Citizens to protect the environment. 
These provisions reflect India's constitutional vision of balancing ecological sustainability with 
social justice. However, in reality, the pendulum swings toward conservation, disregarding 
tribal rights.2 
Environment Statutes: Conservation vs. Livelihood 
• After Independence, several environmental laws were enacted with the goal of 
conservation. Yet these laws often disregarded the traditional practices and rights of 
forest-dwelling communities.  
• The Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 restricts de-reservation of forests and requires 
approval of centre for noon-forest use, which results in bureaucratic hurdles for 
community access. 
• The Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972 created protected areas, but its enforcement evicted 
tribal communities from core zones, ignoring their conservation roles. 
• The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 provides a comprehensive regulatory 
framework but it rarely integrates tribal voices in Environmental Impact Assessments 
(EIAs). 
