The Forest Rights Act, 2006: A Paradigm Shift? 
The Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) 
Act, 2006 (FRA), was a landmark legislation whose aim was to correct historical injustices.4 
Key Provisions: 
• Recognition of individual rights to forest land for habitation and cultivation. 
• Recognition of community rights over the forest produce, grazing, water resources and 
cultural sites. 
• Provision for community forest resource management under Gram Sabhas. 
The FRA was envisioned as a decentralised forest governance model, placing Gram Sabhas at 
the core. However, its implementation has been resisted by forest departments, bureaucratic 
inertia and legal ambiguities.5 
Legal Conflicts and Hierarchies 
Despite the formation of the FRA, conflict between tribal rights and environmental regulation 
still persists. 
Several state governments and courts have questioned the primacy of the FRA over the Forest 
Conservation Act, leading to evictions and the denial of land claims. The lack of a clear legal 
hierarchy creates confusion and injustice. 
The absence of an integrated legal approach has meant that while some laws recognise tribal 
autonomy, others undermine it in the name of ecological urgency. 
