The establishment of protected zones under the Wildlife Protection Act has been a zone of 
Conflict. By making areas wildlife sanctuaries or national parks, the state restricts human 
activities, including those of the indigenous communities. This exclusionary conservation 
model has led to numerous evictions and socio-cultural disruption. 
For example, the eviction of the Soliga Tribe from the Biligiri Rangaswamy Temple Wildlife 
Sanctuary in Karnataka shows how forest-dwelling communities are alienated despite their 
historical ties to the land. These evictions not only displace families but also sever their spiritual 
and cultural ties from their ancestral lands, which destroys tribes and their identities. 
Development-induced Displacement 
Environmental clearance processes under the Environment (Protection) Act and Forest 
(Conservation) Act serve as instruments for granting land for mining, infrastructure and 
hydropower projects. These projects frequently fall in ecologically rich tribal areas, leading to 
massive displacement and loss of livelihood. 
The construction of large dams such as the Sardar Sarovar Project resulted in the displacement 
of thousands of Adivasi Families without adequate rehabilitation, which even led to the deaths 
of many. The affected communities were not consulted, and their consent was never obtained 
despite constitutional provisions and international norms emphasising free, prior, and informed 
consent (FPIC).6 
Criminalisation of Traditional Practices 
Many environmental laws criminalise traditional livelihood activities such as grazing, firewood 
collection, shifting cultivation and the use of minor forest produce. These activities, which were 
community norms, are now punishable under statutory law. 
For example, in Chhattisgarh, forest-dwelling communities were penalised for collecting tendu 
leaves or firewood, which are their daily activities and fully sustainable. This type of legal 
hostility converts traditional custodians into criminals in the eyes of law. 
