to decide on forest land diversion. This landmark case affirmed tribal autonomy under 
the Forest Rights Act.7 
• Hasdeo Arand and Coal Mining Conflicts 
In the Hasdeo Arand Forests of Chhattisgarh, indigenous communities protested against 
state-sanctioned coal mining. Despite the ecological significance of the region and local 
opposition, mining clearances were granted. The movement against mining, led by 
Adivasi groups highlights the disconnect between environmental governance and tribal 
consent.8 
• Aarey Forest in Mumbai 
The proposed construction of a metro shed in the Aarey Forest area sparked widespread 
protests. In which Tribal groups, environmentalists and citizens opposed the project, 
citing ecological and cultural concerns. Though it was initially approved, the project 
faced political reversals due to public pressure, which shows the potential of urban 
indigenous solidarity in environmental movements.9 
• Kaimur Forest Conflict 
In Bihar’s Kaimur region, tribal communities resisted forest eviction drives and filed 
claims under the FRA. This shows how local authorities and forest departments ignore 
legal procedures, underscoring the gaps between the law and its implementation.10 
These case studies show how indigenous rights are negotiated, contested and occasionally 
upheld in India’s environmental governance framework. 
