In India, the judiciary has played a significant role in interpreting laws at the crossroads of 
environmental protection and Indigenous rights. While some judgements have reinforced 
indigenous claims over land and self-governance, others have prioritised conservation or 
economic development at the cost of tribal livelihoods. This duality highlights the complex 
legal terrain navigated by courts in these matters. 
• The Niyamgiri Judgement (Orissa Mining Corporation v. Ministry of 
Environment and Forests, 2013) 
This landmark case revolved around Vedanta’s proposed bauxite mining project in the 
Niyamgiri Hills. The Supreme Court, for the first time, empowered the Gram Sabha to 
decide on the fate of the Project, recognising the spiritual and cultural link of the 
Dongria Kondh tribe with the hills. This judgment affirmed the constitutional validity 
of the Forest Rights Act and underscored the role of local self-governance in 
environmental decisions.11 
• Godavarman Thirumulpad v. Union of India (1996-ongoing) 
This case began as a Public Interest Litigation (PIL) aimed at protecting forests. While 
it has promoted stronger conservation efforts and expanded what we consider a 
"forest," it has unfortunately created significant challenges for tribal communities. The 
restrictions that came with this ruling often conflicted with the Forest Rights Act, 
highlighting a tendency to prioritize environmental concerns over the rights of people 
who have lived in these areas for generations. This situation has left many feeling stuck 
and unheard in their struggle to maintain their livelihoods and way of life.12 
• Banwasi Seva Ashram v. State of Uttar Pradesh (1986) 
This case focused on the difficult situation faced by tribal families who had to leave 
their homes for a thermal power project. The Supreme Court ultimately supported the 
project's development but stressed how vital it is to ensure proper rehabilitation for 
those affected. While the court recognized the vulnerability of these tribal 
communities, it didn't challenge the legitimacy of their displacement. This shows a 
trend in the judiciary that often prioritises development over the rights and well-being 
of marginalised groups.13 
