• Samatha v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1997) 
The court determined that allowing private mining companies to lease tribal land in 
Scheduled Areas went against the Constitution and the PESA Act. It stressed the 
importance of protecting tribal rights and highlighted the need for indigenous 
communities to have control over their own land. Unfortunately, the way these rulings 
are put into practice has often fallen short, which weakens their potential to create real 
change for tribal communities.14 
Judicial interpretations sometimes feel inconsistent, especially when it comes to balancing 
environmental law with the Forest Rights Act. This disconnect has created legal contradictions 
that are confusing and frustrating. While courts often invoke the precautionary principle to 
safeguard the environment, they don't always apply a human rights perspective in cases 
involving indigenous communities, which is concerning.  
Some rulings have indeed set important precedents, bringing attention to these issues. 
However, there have been moments when the judiciary has leaned towards paternalistic or 
conservationist perspectives, sidelining the voices of tribal communities. It’s a reminder of 
how crucial it is to truly listen to and prioritize the experiences and rights of those directly 
affected. 
Comparative Global Analysis  
Learning from International Jurisprudence 
Across the globe, many nations are navigating the complex relationship between 
environmental conservation and the rights of indigenous peoples. A promising trend in 
international environmental law is the move from exclusionary conservation practices to more 
inclusive and community-led approaches. India stands to gain significantly by learning from 
countries that have transformed their environmental policies to empower indigenous 
communities. 
• Canada: Co-Management and Legal Recognition 
