In Canada, various indigenous groups, including the Inuit and First Nations, have achieved 
considerable control over their ancestral lands through co-management agreements and land 
claim settlements. A significant court decision, Delgamuukw v. British Columbia (1997), 
confirmed that aboriginal title encompasses the right to exclusive use and occupation of land. 
Canada’s Constitution Act, 1982, with its Section 35, provides constitutional recognition of 
indigenous rights, further fortifying their legal status. 
An excellent example of this collaborative approach is the Gwaii Haanas National Park 
Reserve, which is managed jointly by the Haida Nation and the Canadian government. This 
partnership not only promotes ecological protection but also honors the cultural and spiritual 
connections that indigenous peoples have with their land.15 
• Australia: Native Title and Environmental Participation 
Australia's Native Title Act of 1993, established following the landmark Mabo decision, 
acknowledges the traditional rights of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples over land 
and sea. This legislation has opened doors for indigenous communities to engage actively in 
environmental management, particularly in biodiversity conservation. 
Programs like Indigenous Protected Areas (IPAs) and the Working on Country initiative 
empower Aboriginal communities to take the lead in conservation. These initiatives recognize 
Indigenous Ecological Knowledge (IEK) and blend it with scientific approaches, fostering 
both employment opportunities and cultural identity. This interplay demonstrates that 
enhancing indigenous rights can advance environmental objectives.16 
• Latin America: Legal Personhood of Nature and Plurinationalism 
In Latin America, countries such as Bolivia and Ecuador have pioneered constitutional 
frameworks that recognize the "Rights of Nature." Ecuador’s 2008 Constitution was a 
groundbreaking move, granting legal rights to ecosystems like rivers and forests. Similarly, 
Bolivia’s Law of Mother Earth acknowledges the inherent rights of nature and emphasizes the 
role of indigenous peoples in sustaining ecological balance. 
