Both nations embrace the concept of plurinationalism, which recognizes the existence of 
multiple nationalities within their borders. In Ecuador and Bolivia, indigenous communities 
are granted constitutional rights to prior consultation, autonomy, and cultural preservation, 
aligning closely with the principles of Free, Prior, and Informed Consent (FPIC).17 
• Africa: Customary Rights and Legal Pluralism 
In many African nations, including Kenya and South Africa, customary land tenure systems 
have been integrated into formal legal structures. A pivotal ruling in the Endorois case by the 
African Commission on Human and Peoples’ Rights determined that the Kenyan government 
had violated the rights of the Endorois people by evicting them from their ancestral lands for 
a game reserve. This case highlighted the cultural and spiritual significance of land, setting a 
vital precedent for indigenous environmental rights in Africa. Similarly, South Africa’s Interim 
Protection of Informal Land Rights Act (IPILRA) upholds communal land tenure, even in the 
absence of formal titles, affirming legal pluralism and oral traditions.18 
Synthesis: Toward a Comparative Framework for India 
From these global examples, several key themes emerge: - Recognition of indigenous legal pluralism and traditional governance systems. - Institutionalisation of community-led conservation initiatives. - Integration of indigenous ecological knowledge into environmental management. - Implementation of legal safeguards, such as FPIC and co-management agreements. 
India's path can greatly benefit from these international best practices. By incorporating 
indigenous perspectives into conservation policies, formalising legal pluralism, and enhancing 
community involvement in environmental decision-making, India can create a more inclusive 
and effective approach to environmental stewardship. 
